    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d525">

                <pb facs="0634" n="1468" sameAs="frus:frus1918Supp01v02/pg1468"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 656.119/426</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at London</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Skinner</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-04-22">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">April 22, 1918</hi>.</date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received May 8.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p rendition="#center">
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Subject</hi>: Ex-President
                    Taft’s memorandum respecting the requisitioning of Dutch
                        ships<anchor n="1" xml:id="d525-f1"/>
                </p>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to refer to the
                    memorandum on the above subject received recently from the Department of State,
                    which I caused to be circulated in various circles in London.<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d525-f2"/> I have to-day learned that the Foreign Office being upon
                    the point of despatching a note to Holland on the legal aspect of the affair and
                        Lord Robert Cecil
                    not having seen Mr. Taft’s opinion, he has asked to be
                    provided with a copy which is now before him, and which no doubt will be of
                    assistance to him in dealing with the matter. Any material of this kind sent to
                    me for distribution is always re-addressed in such manner as to bring about the
                    most useful results.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Robert P. Skinner</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d525-f1" n="1" xml:id="d525-f1-content">
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Ante</hi>, <ref target="#pg1425">p. 1425</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d525-f2" n="2" xml:id="d525-f2-content">
                <p>According to despatches from the Consuls at Halifax and Moncton, the memorandum
                    was communicated by them to local newspapers and published. (File Nos.
                    656.119/331 and 334.)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
